{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018aVrHPEvJUJ8LLKG3Vq92W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"One test failure: `mode_invalid/bogus_mode`. GNU mkfifo outputs `mkfifo: invalid mode\\n` (without showing the bad mode value in quotes), while my code outputs `mkfifo: invalid mode 'bogus'\\n`.\n\nWait, that's interesting. After normalization, the ref stderr is `\"mkfifo: invalid mode\\n\"` and my stderr is `\"mkfifo: invalid mode 'bogus'\\n\"`.\n\nHmm, looking at the normalization: `normalizeBinaryName` replaces binary name patterns. It wouldn't strip the mode value. So the difference is that GNU mkfifo just prints `\"invalid mode\"` without the mode value.\n\n\nLet me double-check what GNU actually outputs here. The normalized ref stderr shows `\"mkfifo: invalid mode\\n\"`, which means the original GNU output was `\"gmkfifo: invalid mode\\n\"`. So it seems GNU doesn't include the invalid mode value in the error message at all.\n\nBut wait, looking at the GNU coreutils source code, it should be outputting the mode value with something like `error (EXIT_FAILURE, 0, _(\"invalid mode %s\"), quote (specified_mode))`. That would produce `gmkfifo: invalid mode 'bogus'`. \n\nComparing the actual outputs, the ref stderr is just `\"mkfifo: invalid mode\\n\"` while the Go implementation outputs `\"mkfifo: invalid mode 'bogus'\\n\"`. So the Go version is including the invalid mode value, but GNU's output in the test doesn't show it.\n\nLet me reconsider what's happening with the normalization and the raw reference output.\n\nThe raw stderr from gmkfifo shows the full error message with the mode value and the help hint, but after normalization it's being reduced to just the first line without the quoted 'bogus' value. This suggests the normalization process might be stripping out the mode value or there's a version difference in how GNU coreutils reports this particular error. Looking at the GNU coreutils so